const e={slug:"runes",title:"The Runes",otherNames:["futhark (fuþark), from its first six letters","Elder (Older) Futhark","Old Norse rúnar","Gothic rūna, “mystery”","Old English rūn, “secret, counsel”"],family:"The runic alphabets of the early Germanic peoples: a script in everyday use, not a learned cipher, which later centuries surrounded with magical lore.",keyedTo:"none",direction:"ltr",status:"ready",summary:"The runes are the native alphabet of the early Germanic peoples. The earliest securely dated inscription is the Vimose comb from Denmark, of about 160 CE. Most of what survives is memorials, ownership marks and messages, but a smaller group seems to have been cut for protection, cursing or healing, and the Eddic poems, written down in thirteenth-century Iceland, present runes as a power that Óðinn won.",intro:["There are three main rows. The Elder Futhark of twenty-four runes was in use until about 700–800; the English expanded it into the futhorc of twenty-eight or more; and the Viking Age cut it down to the sixteen runes of the Younger Futhark. This page sets out the Elder row, the one the others grew from.","Most scholars derive the runes from a Mediterranean alphabet, most often a North Italic (Alpine) one, though Latin and Greek have had their advocates. The word has a history of its own: Gothic rūna translates the Greek mystērion, Old English rūn means “secret, counsel, whisper”, and Old Irish rún, a Celtic cognate, means “secret”. That is evidence about a word, not proof that the letters were secret.","The runes belong in this wing because they were also used, and later imagined, as instruments of power: in the bracteate formulas and the curse stones, in the Edda, in the Latin charms medieval Christians wrote in runes, in the Gothicist Kabbalah of Johannes Bureus, and in the rune systems of the twentieth century. They do not come here through Agrippa. The research behind this page knows of no runic alphabet anywhere in De occulta philosophia, though an absence of that kind cannot be proved by searching, and the wing claims no Agrippan pedigree for them."],firstAttestation:{work:"The Vimose comb, a bone or antler comb inscribed harja (read as a personal name, a tribal name or a word for “comb”; the sense is disputed)",author:"anonymous",date:"c. 160 CE (Rundata; some secondary sources give c. 150)",year:160,place:"Vimose bog, Funen (Fyn), Denmark",locator:"Rundata DR 207 (also Krause–Jankuhn no. 26; DK Fyn 19); National Museum of Denmark, Copenhagen, inv. 22657, as given in the Rundata record",notes:"Several early objects compete for “first”. The Meldorf brooch (Schleswig-Holstein), dated by its type to the first half of the 1st century CE, has four incised signs whose reading is disputed: runic hiwi (Düwel and Gebühr, 1981), Latin IDIN (Odenstedt, 1989; Seebold, 1994) or runic iṛiḷi (Mees, 1997); if they are runic, it is more than a century older than Vimose. The Svingerud stone (Hole, Ringerike, Norway) was announced in January 2023 by the Museum of Cultural History, University of Oslo, which dated it to 1–250 CE from burnt bone and charcoal in the cremation pit and called it the oldest datable runestone; an article in Antiquity (2025) treats the find as fragments of one slab, possibly carved on different occasions, and dates the grave contexts more widely, to between 50 BCE and 275 CE. The first complete row of all twenty-four runes is on the Kylver stone, Gotland (Rundata G 88, c. 400, a date taken from the grave)."},drawing:{face:"TL Runic: the Runic block (U+16A0–16F8) of Noto Sans Runic 2.002 (SIL Open Font License 1.1), via @fontsource/noto-sans-runic 5.3.0",set:"The runes are Unicode characters (the Runic block, U+16A0–16FF), so they are set as text, not drawn. Each code point and Unicode name below was checked mechanically against the Unicode Character Database. The font gives normalised forms close to the Unicode charts; it does not copy any one inscription.",variants:"Where the font departs from the angular forms of the handbook tables, the letter’s note says so. It curves the branches of ᚠ and rounds the shoulder of ᚢ and the bow of ᚦ; it leaves the pocket of ᚱ open towards the stave; it gives ᛊ four strokes where many tables give three; and it draws ᚲ, ᛃ and ᛜ smaller than full height. Early inscriptions vary more than any font, and nothing here should be read as the form of a particular stone."},sample:{orders:[1,2,3,4,5,6],caption:"f · u · þ · a · r · k — the first six runes give the row its name."},lettersTitle:"The Elder Futhark",valueLabel:"Sound",scriptNote:"Twenty-four runes in three groups of eight, the ættir, in the conventional modern order of the handbooks (Barnes 2012; Düwel 2008). That order takes … ï p … from the Vadstena and Grumpan bracteates and the ending … ŋ d o from the Kylver stone, which itself has p before ï; Vadstena ends … ŋ o d, and so does the Old English poem (ēþel, then dæg). The Proto-Germanic names, marked with an asterisk, are modern reconstructions. Only the names in the three medieval rune poems — Old English, Norwegian and Icelandic — are attested, and they often disagree. Runes carry no numbers in the early tradition.",groups:[{key:"1",label:"First ætt",note:"f u þ a r k g w"},{key:"2",label:"Second ætt",note:"h n i j ï p z s"},{key:"3",label:"Third ætt",note:"t b e m l ŋ d o"}],letters:[{order:1,name:"*fehu",gloss:"cattle, movable wealth",value:"F",translit:"f",numeric:null,group:"1",char:"ᚠ",codepoint:"U+16A0",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER FEHU FEOH FE F",formConfidence:"medium",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"feoh, “wealth”",norwegian:"fé, “wealth”",icelandic:"fé, “wealth”"},note:"It survives into the Younger Futhark and the futhorc with the same sound, and all three rune poems name it “wealth”. Its two branches spring from the stave below the top, about a quarter and a half of the way down, and rise to the right; Noto Sans Runic draws them as gentle curves where the handbooks draw them straight."},{order:2,name:"*ūruz",gloss:"aurochs, the wild ox",value:"U",translit:"u",numeric:null,group:"1",char:"ᚢ",codepoint:"U+16A2",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER URUZ UR U",formConfidence:"high",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"ūr, “aurochs”",norwegian:"úr, “slag, dross”",icelandic:"úr, “drizzle, shower”"},note:"The poems disagree about the name: aurochs in the Old English, slag in the Norwegian, drizzle in the Icelandic. The handbook form is angular; Noto rounds the shoulder into an arch."},{order:3,name:"*þurisaz",gloss:"giant (þurs)",value:"Þ",translit:"þ",numeric:null,group:"1",char:"ᚦ",codepoint:"U+16A6",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER THURISAZ THURS THORN",formConfidence:"high",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"þorn, “thorn”",norwegian:"þurs, “giant”",icelandic:"þurs, “giant”"},note:"This is the þurs-rune Skírnir threatens to carve in Skírnismál 36 (Neckel–Kuhn; below, “In their words”). The Norwegian poem says þurs causes the sickness of women; the Icelandic calls it the torment of women. It writes the sound th. The handbook form has a pointed, triangular bow at mid-height; Noto draws a large rounded one."},{order:4,name:"*ansuz",gloss:"a god, one of the Æsir",value:"A",translit:"a",numeric:null,group:"1",char:"ᚨ",codepoint:"U+16A8",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER ANSUZ A",formConfidence:"high",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"ōs (given in the futhorc to a new rune, ᚩ)",norwegian:"óss, “river-mouth”",icelandic:"óss, “god”"},note:"In the Anglo-Saxon futhorc this form split into ōs ᚩ, āc ᚪ and æsc ᚫ; in the Younger Futhark the rune stands for a nasal a or o. Its two branches start at the top of the stave and slope down; those of fehu, the f-rune, rise."},{order:5,name:"*raidō",gloss:"riding, journey",value:"R",translit:"r",numeric:null,group:"1",char:"ᚱ",codepoint:"U+16B1",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER RAIDO RAD REID R",formConfidence:"high",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"rād, “riding”",norwegian:"ræið, “riding”",icelandic:"reið, “riding”"},note:"All three poems agree on the name. Its shape is an angular capital R: a stave with a triangular pocket and a leg running down to the baseline. Many inscriptions close the pocket; Noto leaves it open towards the stave."},{order:6,name:"*kaunan (or *kenaz)",gloss:"ulcer, sore (Norse); torch (Old English)",value:"K",translit:"k",numeric:null,group:"1",char:"ᚲ",codepoint:"U+16B2",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER KAUNA",formConfidence:"medium",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"cēn, “torch”",norwegian:"kaun, “sore, ulcer”",icelandic:"kaun, “sore”"},note:"Both names are reconstructions, and the handbooks differ on which to print. In the oldest inscriptions it is a small angle, often set high in the band; Noto centres it just above mid-height, and many fonts draw it full height."},{order:7,name:"*gebō",gloss:"gift",value:"G",translit:"g",numeric:null,group:"1",char:"ᚷ",codepoint:"U+16B7",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER GEBO GYFU G",formConfidence:"high",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"gyfu, “gift”",norwegian:"not in the Younger row",icelandic:"not in the Younger row"},note:"The Younger Futhark dropped it, so only the Old English poem has a stanza for it. Two full-height diagonals crossing at the centre, with no stave."},{order:8,name:"*wunjō",gloss:"joy",value:"W",translit:"w",numeric:null,group:"1",char:"ᚹ",codepoint:"U+16B9",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER WUNJO WYNN W",formConfidence:"high",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"wynn, “joy”",norwegian:"not in the Younger row",icelandic:"not in the Younger row"},note:"The Anglo-Saxon wynn passed into Old English book-hand as the letter ƿ, which scribes used for w. The Younger row dropped it."},{order:9,name:"*hagalaz",gloss:"hail",value:"H",translit:"h",numeric:null,group:"2",char:"ᚺ",codepoint:"U+16BA",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER HAGLAZ H",formConfidence:"high",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"hægl, “hail”",norwegian:"hagall, “hail”",icelandic:"hagall, “hail”"},note:"Two staves joined by one diagonal bar. The English futhorc’s hægl has two parallel bars (ᚻ, U+16BB), and the Younger Futhark uses other forms, ᚼ and ᚽ."},{order:10,name:"*naudiz",gloss:"need, constraint",value:"N",translit:"n",numeric:null,group:"2",char:"ᚾ",codepoint:"U+16BE",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER NAUDIZ NYD NAUD N",formConfidence:"high",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"nȳd, “need”",norwegian:"nauðr, “constraint”",icelandic:"nauð, “need”"},note:"Sigrdrífumál 7 tells the reader to mark Nauð on the fingernail as one of the ale-runes (below, “In their words”). A stave crossed at mid-height by one short sloping stroke."},{order:11,name:"*īsaz",gloss:"ice",value:"I",translit:"i",numeric:null,group:"2",char:"ᛁ",codepoint:"U+16C1",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER ISAZ IS ISS I",formConfidence:"high",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"īs, “ice”",norwegian:"íss, “ice”",icelandic:"íss, “ice”"},note:"A single full-height stave. All three poems agree on its name: ice."},{order:12,name:"*jēra",gloss:"year, a good harvest",value:"J",translit:"j",numeric:null,group:"2",char:"ᛃ",codepoint:"U+16C3",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER JERAN J",formConfidence:"medium",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"gēr, “year”",norwegian:"ár, “plenty, good year”",icelandic:"ár, “plenty”"},note:"Proto-Norse lost initial j-, so *jāra became ár and the rune came to write a in the Younger Futhark. The English gēr has its own form, ᛄ. Early inscriptions vary more on this rune than on any other; Noto draws its two hooks small and set high."},{order:13,name:"*ī(h)waz or *eihwaz",gloss:"yew",value:"Ï",translit:"ï",numeric:null,group:"2",char:"ᛇ",codepoint:"U+16C7",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER IWAZ EOH",formConfidence:"medium",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"ēoh, “yew”",norwegian:"not in the Younger row (the yew-name ýr passed to the z/R-rune)",icelandic:"not in the Younger row"},note:"Its sound — a vowel somewhere near i or e, written ï by convention — is one of the open questions of runology. The yew-name later passed to a different rune (no. 15). The transliterator writes it only when ï itself is typed, since no plain Latin letter answers to it."},{order:14,name:"*perþ-",gloss:"meaning unknown",value:"P",translit:"p",numeric:null,group:"2",char:"ᛈ",codepoint:"U+16C8",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER PERTHO PEORTH P",formConfidence:"medium",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"peorð, meaning unknown",norwegian:"not in the Younger row",icelandic:"not in the Younger row"},note:"The rarest rune of the row, almost absent from early inscriptions. Guesses at the meaning of peorð include a dice-cup, a pear tree and a game; none is established. Its form varies between inscriptions and tables; Noto’s stave with two notched arms turned away from each other agrees with the published descriptions."},{order:15,name:"*algiz",gloss:"elk? protection? (the reconstruction is uncertain)",value:"Z",translit:"z",numeric:null,group:"2",char:"ᛉ",codepoint:"U+16C9",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER ALGIZ EOLHX",formConfidence:"high",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"eolhx, eolh-secg, “elk-sedge”",norwegian:"ýr, “yew” (the inverted form ᛦ)",icelandic:"ýr, “bow”"},note:"It wrote the final -z of Proto-Germanic, later a palatal r. Modern writers call it the “life rune”; as such it was used in National Socialist Germany, by the Lebensborn organisation and, upright and inverted, for dates of birth and death in obituaries and on gravestones (see “What is claimed”). The rune poems give no support to the popular reading “protection”."},{order:16,name:"*sōwilō",gloss:"sun",value:"S",translit:"s",numeric:null,group:"2",char:"ᛊ",codepoint:"U+16CA",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER SOWILO S",formConfidence:"medium",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"sigel, “sun”",norwegian:"sól, “sun”",icelandic:"sól, “sun”"},note:"Inscriptions give it anything from three to about eight strokes, in either orientation, so there is no single correct drawing: Noto has four equal strokes, many handbook tables three. The Younger s-rune is ᛋ or ᛌ. The SS emblem is described as a doubled “Sig” rune, a name taken from List’s Armanen row; its zigzag resembles this rune (see “What is claimed”)."},{order:17,name:"*tīwaz",gloss:"the god Tiw, Týr",value:"T",translit:"t",numeric:null,group:"3",char:"ᛏ",codepoint:"U+16CF",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER TIWAZ TIR TYR T",formConfidence:"high",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"Tīr (in the poem a guiding star or sign)",norwegian:"Týr, the god",icelandic:"Týr, the one-handed god"},note:"Sigrdrífumál 6 tells the reader to carve victory-runes on the sword-hilt and to name Týr twice (ok nefna tysvar Tý; below, “In their words”), and many scholars connect the line with this rune. An upward arrow: a stave with two short strokes falling from its top."},{order:18,name:"*berkanan",gloss:"birch twig",value:"B",translit:"b",numeric:null,group:"3",char:"ᛒ",codepoint:"U+16D2",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER BERKANAN BEORC BJARKAN B",formConfidence:"high",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"beorc, “birch” or “poplar”",norwegian:"bjarkan, “birch twig”",icelandic:"bjarkan, “birch twig”"},note:"An angular capital B: a stave with two closed triangular bows. Early forms sometimes separate the bows slightly or round them."},{order:19,name:"*ehwaz",gloss:"horse",value:"E",translit:"e",numeric:null,group:"3",char:"ᛖ",codepoint:"U+16D6",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER EHWAZ EH E",formConfidence:"high",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"eh, “horse”",norwegian:"not in the Younger row",icelandic:"not in the Younger row"},note:"An angular M with a shallow middle that does not reach the baseline. The Younger row dropped it."},{order:20,name:"*mannaz",gloss:"man, human being",value:"M",translit:"m",numeric:null,group:"3",char:"ᛗ",codepoint:"U+16D7",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER MANNAZ MAN M",formConfidence:"high",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"mann, “man”",norwegian:"maðr, “man”",icelandic:"maðr, “man”"},note:"Two staves with an X between them in the upper half. The Younger Futhark writes m with a different form, ᛘ or ᛙ."},{order:21,name:"*laguz (or *laukaz)",gloss:"water, lake (or leek)",value:"L",translit:"l",numeric:null,group:"3",char:"ᛚ",codepoint:"U+16DA",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER LAUKAZ LAGU LOGR L",formConfidence:"high",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"lagu, “water, sea”",norwegian:"lǫgr, “water, waterfall”",icelandic:"lǫgr, “water”"},note:"Some runologists reconstruct the Elder name as *laukaz, “leek”, because laukaz is a common word on the bracteates; it is a disputed proposal, and all three poems give the water-name."},{order:22,name:"*ingwaz",gloss:"the god Ing (Yngvi)",value:"Ŋ",translit:"ŋ",numeric:null,group:"3",char:"ᛜ",codepoint:"U+16DC",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER INGWAZ",formConfidence:"medium",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"Ing, a hero or god, in the poem first seen among the East Danes",norwegian:"not in the Younger row",icelandic:"not in the Younger row"},note:"It writes the sound ng. Its size and position vary between the early rows: usually a small diamond set in the upper part or the middle of the band, but sometimes full height or on a stave. Noto centres it at mid-height, about half the band high."},{order:23,name:"*dagaz",gloss:"day",value:"D",translit:"d",numeric:null,group:"3",char:"ᛞ",codepoint:"U+16DE",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER DAGAZ DAEG D",formConfidence:"high",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"dæg, “day”",norwegian:"not in the Younger row",icelandic:"not in the Younger row"},note:"On Kylver d comes before o; on some bracteate rows, and in the Old English poem, it comes after (ēþel, then dæg). Two staves joined by two crossing diagonals."},{order:24,name:"*ōþalan or *ōþila",gloss:"inherited land, ancestral estate",value:"O",translit:"o",numeric:null,group:"3",char:"ᛟ",codepoint:"U+16DF",unicodeName:"RUNIC LETTER OTHALAN ETHEL O",formConfidence:"high",poemNames:{oldEnglish:"ēþel, “homeland, estate”",norwegian:"not in the Younger row",icelandic:"not in the Younger row"},note:"The “odal rune”. The SS used it as an emblem — which of its offices and units did so is widely stated but was not confirmed in the research for this page — and a variant with serifs or “wings” appears in post-war far-right iconography. The plain letter shown here is the historical form; the variants are not reproduced."}],transliteration:{from:"latin",convention:"This writes modern spelling letter by letter in the Elder Futhark. It is the site’s convention, not an ancient practice: the carvers spelled by sound in their own languages, never by English letters, and a real inscription of an English word would look different. The folds below are applied in the order given, and every one used is reported under the result.",folds:[{from:"th",to:"þ",note:"the sound th has its own rune, þurisaz"},{from:"ng",to:"ŋ",note:"the sound ng has its own rune, ingwaz"},{from:"c",to:"k",note:"the row has no c"},{from:"q",to:"k",note:"the row has no q"},{from:"x",to:"ks",note:"written as its two sounds"},{from:"v",to:"w",note:"the row has no v; wunjō is the nearest"},{from:"y",to:"j",note:"the row has no y; jēra writes the y of “year”"}],unwritten:"ᛇ (ï) is written only when ï itself is typed: no plain Latin letter answers to it, and its sound is disputed."},transmission:[{slug:"vimose",title:"The earliest inscriptions",kind:"discovery",year:160,end:260,circa:!0,circaEnd:!0,description:"The first securely runic texts — the Vimose comb (DR 207) and the Øvre Stabu spearhead from Norway (N KJ31), inscribed raunijaz — are short: names, makers’ or owners’ words, the names of weapons.",dating:"Rundata dates the Vimose comb c. 160; the Øvre Stabu spearhead is dated between about 160 and 260, depending on the source. The Svingerud fragments (1–250 CE in the announcement of 2023; grave contexts of 50 BCE–275 CE in Antiquity, 2025) and the disputed Meldorf brooch leave an earlier start open."},{slug:"kylver",title:"The Kylver stone: the first complete row",kind:"discovery",year:400,end:null,circa:!0,circaEnd:!1,description:"A limestone slab from a grave at Kylver, Stånga parish, Gotland (Rundata G 88), excavated in 1903 and now in the Swedish History Museum, Stockholm. It carries all twenty-four runes in a fixed order, followed by a tree-like sign that some read as stacked t-runes; the palindrome sueus stands apart from the row. It is the earliest evidence that the row was learned as a sequence. The inscription was on the underside of the slab, facing into the grave; reading it as a message for the dead is an interpretation, which the inscription neither confirms nor refutes.",dating:"c. 400, a date taken from the grave. Its row reads … j p ï z … ŋ d o: p before ï and d before o, the reverse of both pairs on the Vadstena bracteate."},{slug:"bracteates",title:"The gold bracteates",kind:"practice",year:400,end:600,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,when:"5th–6th centuries",description:"Gold pendants modelled on Roman imperial medallions. Vadstena (Ög 178; found 1774, c. 500; stolen from the Swedish History Museum in 1938 and never recovered) and Grumpan (Vg 207; found 1911, 6th century) divide the futhark with dots into three groups of eight, the later ættir. Others carry short formulas such as alu, laþu, laukaz and auja. They are the centre of the debate on “rune magic”."},{slug:"blekinge",title:"The Blekinge curse stones",kind:"text",year:500,end:700,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,when:"6th–7th centuries (dating debated)",description:"Stentoften (DR 357) and Björketorp (DR 360), in the transitional runic of southern Sweden, threaten whoever breaks the monument and name runes of power (ginArunAz) hidden there.",dating:"Björketorp is usually dated to the 6th or 7th century. Hanna Åkerström (Futhark, 2025) argues that the usual reading order of Stentoften should be revised."},{slug:"noleby",title:"Runes “derived from the gods”",kind:"text",year:600,end:800,circa:!0,circaEnd:!0,when:"c. 600 and c. 800",description:"The Noleby stone (Vg 63, c. 600) and the Sparlösa stone (Vg 119, c. 800), both in Västergötland, call runes raginaku(n)do and rægi[n]kundu, “derived from the gods”. Hávamál 80 (in Neckel–Kuhn’s numbering) uses the same word, reginkunnum, centuries later.",dating:"Bellows orders Hávamál 79 and 80 differently and numbers this stanza 79."},{slug:"oe-rune-poem",title:"The Old English Rune Poem",kind:"text",year:600,end:1e3,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,when:"composed between the 7th and the 10th century (estimates differ); printed 1705",description:"Twenty-nine stanzas, one for each rune of the futhorc, each beginning with the rune’s name: Christian, gnomic and mnemonic. Its manuscript, London, British Library, Cotton Otho B.x (fols 165a–165b), was lost in the Ashburnham House fire of 1731. The text survives only through Humfrey Wanley’s copy, printed by George Hickes in his Linguarum veterum septentrionalium thesaurus (Oxford, 1703–05), vol. I (1705), p. 135; since the early 20th century some scholars have questioned how faithfully that print reproduces the lost manuscript."},{slug:"younger",title:"The Younger Futhark",kind:"event",year:700,end:800,circa:!0,circaEnd:!0,description:"The row is cut to sixteen runes, each covering several sounds, in long-branch and short-twig forms. The Rök stone (Ög 136), of the early 9th century, is with about 760 runes the longest runic inscription on stone; parts of it are enciphered, by substituting runes and with special cipher runes."},{slug:"futhorc",title:"The Anglo-Saxon futhorc",kind:"practice",year:700,end:1e3,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,when:"8th–10th centuries",description:"The English expanded the row to twenty-eight runes, adding ōs, āc, æsc, ȳr, īor, ēar and others. It is cut on the Franks Casket (8th century, Northumbria), the Ruthwell Cross and the Seax of Beagnoth, or Thames scramasax (10th century, according to the British Museum), which has the only complete row of twenty-eight, in an irregular order. The poet Cynewulf signed four poems with runes: Elene and The Fates of the Apostles in the Vercelli Book, Juliana and Christ II in the Exeter Book."},{slug:"ribe",title:"The Ribe skull fragment",kind:"discovery",year:725,end:750,circa:!0,circaEnd:!1,when:"c. 725 (deposited 725–750, by a dating study of 2013)",description:"A piece of human skull with a hole bored through it, inscribed with the names Ulfr, Óðinn and Hō-Tīwʀ, “High-Týr”. The older reading takes them as help against pain and a “dwarf”, a disease-spirit; Jackie Nordström (2021) reads help against a male and a female dwarf, and Staffan Fridell (2024) disputes the dwarf reading. It is the clearest early protective or healing charm, but its wording is contested."},{slug:"abecedarium",title:"Carolingian rune-lists",kind:"manuscript",year:800,end:900,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,when:"9th century",description:"The Abecedarium Nordmannicum (St Gallen, Stiftsbibliothek, Cod. Sang. 878, p. 321) names the sixteen Younger runes in a mixture of Old Saxon, Old High German and Norse; the page was damaged in the 19th century by chemicals meant to preserve it, and its text is known partly from Wilhelm Grimm’s drawing of 1828. Vienna, ÖNB Cod. 795 preserves names for the letters of the Gothic alphabet, in a language that differs from Wulfila’s, and some scholars doubt how far they can be relied on. With the rune poems, these learned records are the basis for reconstructing the Elder names."},{slug:"norwegian-poem",title:"The Norwegian Rune Poem",kind:"text",year:1200,end:null,circa:!0,circaEnd:!1,when:"probably early 13th century",description:"Sixteen couplets on the Younger runes, each pairing the rune’s name with an unrelated proverb. The medieval manuscript was lost in the Copenhagen fire of 1728; the poem survives through Ole Worm’s print in Runir (1636) and late 17th-century copies, the most authoritative by Árni Magnússon and Jón Eggertsson."},{slug:"egils-saga",title:"Egils saga",kind:"text",year:1220,end:1240,circa:!0,circaEnd:!0,description:"In the saga, Egill carves runes and reddens them with his blood so that a poisoned horn bursts (ch. 44); carves runes on a scorn-pole (níðstöng) to curse King Eiríkr and Queen Gunnhildr (ch. 57); and cures a sick girl by scraping off a botched rune charm on whalebone and carving a new one (ch. 72). These are literary episodes, written about two centuries after the events they describe.",dating:"Written in the second quarter of the 13th century; the main manuscript is Möðruvallabók (AM 132 fol., c. 1330–70). Chapters as in Íslenzk fornrit II; W. C. Green’s translation (1893) puts the horn in ch. 45. Many scholars attribute the saga to Snorri Sturluson on grounds of style, as Björn M. Ólsen argued systematically in 1904; the attribution is debated."},{slug:"codex-regius",title:"The Codex Regius of the Poetic Edda",kind:"manuscript",year:1270,end:null,circa:!0,circaEnd:!1,description:"Reykjavík, Árni Magnússon Institute, GKS 2365 4to, in Copenhagen until its return to Iceland on 21 April 1971. It is the only witness to Hávamál, where Óðinn tells how he hung nine nights on the tree and took up the runes (the Rúnatal, stanzas 138–145 in Neckel–Kuhn) and lists eighteen charms (the Ljóðatal, 146–163). It also holds Sigrdrífumál, which lists the kinds of runes and where to carve them, and Skírnismál, with its threat of a þurs-rune curse. It is the main literary source for rune magic — and late, and poetic.",dating:"Some editions number the Hávamál stanzas 139–146 and 147–165; stanza 164 is often taken as a later addition. Editors divide the stanzas of the Skírnismál passage differently, so its stanza number varies."},{slug:"icelandic-poem",title:"The Icelandic Rune Poem",kind:"text",year:1500,end:1550,circa:!0,circaEnd:!0,when:"c. 1500 and later (manuscripts)",description:"Sixteen short stanzas of kennings, recorded in four Arnamagnæan manuscripts, among them AM 687d 4to (c. 1500), whose central part is badly damaged, and AM 461 12mo (c. 1550). In the standard editions (Dickins 1915; Page 1999) each stanza is followed by a Latin word and a Norse word for “chieftain” that alliterates with the rune’s name. Those glosses could not be checked for this page, so none is given here."},{slug:"bureus",title:"Johannes Bureus and the Adulrunes",kind:"person",year:1599,end:1649,circa:!1,circaEnd:!0,when:"1599 to the 1640s",description:"Johannes Bureus (Johan Bure, 1568–1652), the Swedish royal antiquary, recorded runestones and published the engraved runic sheet Runakänslanes lärespån (Elementa runica, 1599) and a Runa ABC (1611). He also built a Gothicist, Kabbalistic and Hermetic system of fifteen “Adulrunes”, which he called a Kabala Upsalica or Notaricon Suethica, and worked it out over some forty years in several versions of the manuscript Adulruna rediviva. A study of Kabbalah in Sweden names Agrippa, Paracelsus and Reuchlin among his models, and he read John Dee’s Monas hieroglyphica in 1610. His is the first learned rune esotericism, and the closest point of contact between the runes and the Christian-Kabbalist tradition of the rest of this wing (Karlsson 2010; Åkerman 1998)."},{slug:"worm",title:"Ole Worm’s Runir",kind:"text",year:1636,end:1643,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,when:"1636; 1643",description:"Runir seu Danica literatura antiquissima (Copenhagen, 1636; 2nd ed. 1651) printed runic alphabets and texts, the Norwegian Rune Poem among them, and Danicorum monumentorum libri sex (1643) published the Danish runestones. With Bureus’s work in Sweden, these books made runic monuments a subject of Latin scholarship."},{slug:"list",title:"Guido von List’s “Armanen” runes",kind:"text",year:1906,end:1908,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,when:"1906; 1908",description:"In Das Geheimnis der Runen — a periodical article in 1906, then a book in 1908, no. 1 of the Guido-von-List-Bücherei — Guido von List (1848–1919) introduced an eighteen-rune “Armanen” row. List claimed that it came to him in a revelation during some eleven months of blindness after a cataract operation in 1902, and he keyed it to the eighteen charms of Hávamál’s Ljóðatal. Historians treat it as a modern creation; it is the root of most völkisch and later occult rune systems."},{slug:"volkisch",title:"Völkisch rune occultism and National Socialist use",kind:"event",year:1920,end:1945,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,when:"1920s to 1945",description:"“Rune gymnastics” and “rune yoga” (F. B. Marby, S. A. Kummer) developed out of List. In the early 1930s the SS adopted as its emblem a doubled “Sig” rune drawn by Walter Heck (in 1929 or 1933; the sources differ), and it also used the odal rune and the “life” and “death” runes; K. M. Wiligut, Himmler’s adviser, designed the SS Totenkopfring with runic signs. Runology itself was entangled with the regime (Mees 2008; Goodrick-Clarke 1985)."},{slug:"blum",title:"Ralph Blum’s Book of Runes",kind:"text",year:1982,end:null,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,when:"1982 (some records give 1983)",description:"The Book of Runes (New York: Oracle Books / St. Martin’s Press), a rune-casting oracle sold with a set of rune tiles, adds a blank rune called “Odin” and reorders the row. It is the source of much present-day divinatory rune meaning, and it is modern.",dating:"Records disagree on the year of the first edition, and whether the blank rune is already in it was not checked against a copy."},{slug:"unicode",title:"The Runic block in Unicode",kind:"event",year:1999,end:2014,circa:!1,circaEnd:!1,when:"1999; 2014",description:"Unicode 3.0 encoded the Elder, Anglo-Saxon, Younger and medieval runes in one block, U+16A0–16FF, with the calendar signs arlaug, tvímaðr and belgþor (U+16EE–16F0) for the golden numbers 17–19; Unicode 7.0 added U+16F1–16F8, Tolkien’s runes and the Franks Casket variants."}],claims:[{claim:"The runes were adapted from a Mediterranean alphabet, most probably a North Italic (Alpine) one of the Etruscan family.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"Several letter shapes and the free direction of writing, left or right, match the North Italic alphabets, and that thesis — Carl Marstrander’s, in the first volume of Norsk tidsskrift for sprogvidenskap (1928) — is the most widely held. Ludvig Wimmer (Runeskriftens oprindelse, 1874) derived the runes from the Latin capitals. Otto von Friesen (from 1904–06) and Sophus Bugge argued for a Greek script borrowed by the Goths, which the dates make hard to sustain; Bugge had earlier favoured a North Etruscan source. There is no consensus.",sources:["Barnes 2012","Düwel 2008","Page 1987"]},{claim:"The word “rune” meant “secret, mystery” before it meant “letter”.",standing:"documented",evidence:"Gothic rūna translates Greek mystērion in Wulfila’s Bible (Mark 4:11); Old English rūn means “secret, counsel, whisper”, and Old Irish rún, a Celtic cognate, “secret”. This is evidence about a word. It does not show that the letters were themselves secret or magical.",sources:["Page 1987","Barnes 2012"]},{claim:"Hávamál tells that Óðinn won the runes by hanging nine nights on a wind-swept tree, wounded by a spear and given to Óðinn, himself to himself.",standing:"legend",evidence:"Hávamál 138–139 in Neckel–Kuhn’s numbering (139–140 in Bellows, who moves the manuscript’s closing stanza ahead of the Rúnatal), in the Codex Regius (GKS 2365 4to, c. 1270), the poem’s only witness. Its date of composition is unknown, and it was written down in Christian Iceland.",sources:["Codex Regius, GKS 2365 4to","Neckel–Kuhn, Edda","Bellows 1923","Pettit 2023"]},{claim:"Runes were held to be of divine origin.",standing:"documented",evidence:"Some carvers say so themselves. The Noleby stone (Vg 63, c. 600) reads runo fahi raginakudo (also transcribed raginaku(n)do), in the site’s paraphrase “I paint the rune derived from the gods”, and Sparlösa (Vg 119, c. 800) has rúnaR … rægi[n]kundu. Hávamál 80 (Neckel–Kuhn) later uses the same word, reginkunnum. Only Cleasby–Vigfússon glossed it otherwise, as “universally known”. The belief is documented; the origin itself is legend.",sources:["Rundata","Düwel 2008","Barnes 2012","Mees, “The Noleby inscription and Germanic *rehaną”"]},{claim:"The word alu, frequent on bracteates and other early objects, is a magical or protective formula.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"alu appears alone or in formulas on many 5th–6th-century bracteates, on the Elgesem stone (Vestfold, Norway; found 1870; Rundata N KJ57, c. 400) and on the Lindholm amulet (Skåne). Proposed meanings include “ale” (Old Norse ǫl, Old English ealu), perhaps a ritual drink, and “protection” or “sanctuary”. Most runologists accept that it was a cultic or amuletic word; they do not agree on its sense.",sources:["MacLeod & Mees 2006","Looijenga 2003","Düwel 2008","Flowers 1986"]},{claim:"The word laukaz, “leek”, on bracteates is a fertility or prosperity charm.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"laukaz and abbreviations of it occur on many bracteates. The Fløksand find (Nordhordland, Norway; a knife or scraper from a woman’s grave, found 1864) reads lina laukaz, “linen [and] leek” (some transcribe laukar). Literary parallels are cited: in Völsa þáttr, preserved only in Flateyjarbók, a preserved horse-phallus is wrapped in linen with leeks and herbs (discussed by Wilhelm Heizmann and Klaus Düwel in Völsi-Geschichten, 2021); Sigrdrífumál 8 tells the reader to throw a leek into the cup; and Egill’s verse calls the sick girl lauka lindi, “linden of leeks”. That the word was written on amulets is documented; that it worked as a charm is an interpretation.",sources:["MacLeod & Mees 2006","Düwel 2008","Flowers 1986"]},{claim:"Futhark rows written out on objects (Kylver, the Vadstena and Grumpan bracteates) were magical in purpose.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"Some scholars read a complete row as an amulet, like the Latin abecedaria on late-antique amulets; others as a writing exercise or a display of literacy. Kylver’s grave context, the inscription on the underside of the slab facing into the grave, is sometimes read in favour of the first view for that stone, but the inscription itself neither confirms nor refutes it.",sources:["Page 1987","MacLeod & Mees 2006","Düwel 2008"]},{claim:"Runes were used for curses on monuments.",standing:"documented",evidence:"Björketorp (DR 360) and Stentoften (DR 357) threaten whoever breaks the stone. Glavendrup (DR 209, Funen, early 10th century) reads Þórr vígi þessi rúnaR, “may Thor hallow these runes”, and ends, in the National Museum of Denmark’s reading, with a curse on whoever damages the stone or drags it away to stand in memory of another. Other Danish stones of the 10th century use the formula, some naming the monument instead of the runes.",sources:["Rundata","MacLeod & Mees 2006","Barnes 2012","National Museum of Denmark, “Runic magic”"]},{claim:"Runes were used in healing charms.",standing:"documented",evidence:"The Ribe skull fragment (c. 725) invokes Ulfr, Óðinn and Hō-Tīwʀ; whether against pain and a dwarf, or against a male and a female dwarf, is debated (Nordström 2021; Fridell 2024). The Sigtuna amulet I (copper, 11th century, found 1931) is a charm against wound-fever; it addresses a þurs (or Þórr: the reading is disputed) and a “wolf”, Ulfr, the name of the person it was made for (Hall 2009). Many medieval lead amulets carry runic charms. Egils saga ch. 72 (Íslenzk fornrit II) describes a healing charm on whalebone, but that is literature, not evidence of practice.",sources:["MacLeod & Mees 2006","Rundata","Nordström 2021","Fridell 2024","Hall 2009"]},{claim:"Medieval Christian Scandinavians wrote learned Latin and Hebrew-derived charms in runes.",standing:"documented",evidence:"The Ave Maria, the Pater Noster, the SATOR square and AGLA appear on runic amulets and on sticks from Bryggen in Bergen (12th–14th centuries). The Bryggen stick N 636 begins agla and goes on with SATOR AREPO and the names of the archangels Raphael, Gabriel and Michael. This is where the runes meet the Latin magical tradition the rest of this wing follows.",sources:["MacLeod & Mees 2006","Spurkland 2005","Rundata N 636"]},{claim:"Egils saga tells that Egill Skalla-Grímsson cured a girl by re-carving runes that an unskilled man had cut wrongly on a whalebone.",standing:"legend",evidence:"Egils saga (written c. 1220–40), ch. 72 in Íslenzk fornrit II; other translations may number the chapters differently. The saga tells the episode with a skaldic verse attributed to Egill (Skaldic Project: Egill, Lausavísur 40). It shows what 13th-century Icelanders thought about runes, not what 10th-century people did.",sources:["Egils saga, Íslenzk fornrit II (1933)","Skaldic Project, Egill Lv 40"]},{claim:"Tacitus describes the Germans casting runes for divination.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"Germania 10 (98 CE) describes a branch of a fruit-bearing tree cut into slips, marked with certain signs (notis quibusdam) and scattered on a white cloth, from which three are taken up and read. Tacitus does not say what the signs were. No inscription confirms runic lots, and the earliest runes are about half a century later than Tacitus.",sources:["Tacitus, Germania 10","Page 1987","Barnes 2012"]},{claim:"The Elder Futhark has known traditional names and meanings for each rune.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"No Elder name-list survives. The names are reconstructed from the three rune poems, the Abecedarium Nordmannicum and the Gothic letter-names in Vienna, ÖNB Cod. 795; several are uncertain (*perþ-, *algiz, *kaunan or *kenaz, *laguz or *laukaz). The poems’ meanings are medieval and often differ from one another.",sources:["Dickins 1915","Halsall 1981","Page 1999","Barnes 2012"]},{claim:"The rune poems are magical texts.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"Their form is mnemonic and gnomic: a name, then a proverb or a kenning. The Old English poem is explicitly Christian; its first stanza speaks of winning glory before the Lord. Modern occult use treats the poems as keys to the meanings of the runes, but nothing in them describes magical use.",sources:["Dickins 1915","Halsall 1981","Page 1999"]},{claim:"erilaz, a title on several early inscriptions (ek erilaz …), means “rune-master” or “magician”.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"The word has been connected with the Heruli, with the rank of jarl and with a professional class of carvers. Its sense is unknown.",sources:["Düwel 2008","Looijenga 2003","MacLeod & Mees 2006"]},{claim:"List claimed that the eighteen Armanen runes were revealed to him and preserved a lost Aryan priestly wisdom.",standing:"attributed",evidence:"This is List’s own claim, in Das Geheimnis der Runen (article 1906; book 1908): a revelation during about eleven months of blindness after a cataract operation in 1902, as Flowers reports in the introduction to his translation (1988). No inscription or manuscript uses the row. It reworks the sixteen Younger runes with two additions (eh, gibor) and keys them to the charms of Hávamál’s Ljóðatal. Historians (Goodrick-Clarke) treat it as a modern creation within Ariosophy.",sources:["List 1908 (trans. Flowers 1988)","Goodrick-Clarke 1985"]},{claim:"The blank “Odin” rune and the standard modern divinatory meanings are ancient.",standing:"legend",evidence:"The blank rune is generally credited to Ralph Blum’s The Book of Runes (1982; some records give 1983), which was sold with rune tiles including a blank “Odin” tile; that it first appears there rests on secondary and popular sources and was not checked against a first edition. Divinatory meanings come from 20th-century occult writers working from the medieval rune poems. No ancient source describes divination with individual runes.",sources:["Blum 1982","Page 1987"]},{claim:"The SS and other National Socialist bodies used runes as insignia.",standing:"documented",evidence:"The doubled “Sig” rune of the SS (drawn by Walter Heck, in 1929 or 1933; the sources differ); the odal rune; and the “life” and “death” runes (ᛉ upright and inverted), used for the Lebensborn and, in obituaries and on gravestones, to mark dates of birth and death in place of the asterisk and the cross. These uses drew on List’s tradition. In Germany the display of Nazi emblems was first banned under Allied occupation law after 1945, and the Assembly Act of 1953 penalised showing the emblems of former National Socialist organisations; the ban entered the Criminal Code as §96a in 1960, has been §86a since 1968, and covers the SS runes. The odal and “life” runes also appear in far-right iconography today, and the same letters are used without such meaning by historians, by modern Heathen religious groups and in ordinary Scandinavian use.",sources:["Goodrick-Clarke 1985","Mees 2008","Deutscher Bundestag, Wissenschaftliche Dienste (2021)"]},{claim:"Runes were mainly a magical script.",standing:"disputed",evidence:"Against: most of the corpus is memorial stones, ownership and makers’ marks and, at Bryggen, business letters, love notes and jokes; in the 6th century Venantius Fortunatus mentions runes painted on ash tablets as a plain way to send a letter (Carmina VII.18). For: amuletic formulas, curse stones and healing charms do exist. The fair statement is that runes were an ordinary script that was also used for magic.",sources:["Page 1964; Page 1987","Spurkland 2005","MacLeod & Mees 2006","Flowers 1986"]}],uses:[{use:"Everyday writing: memorials, ownership and makers’ marks, letters and messages",evidence:"The bulk of the corpus: Viking-Age memorial stones in Sweden, Denmark and Norway; the Bryggen sticks of Bergen (12th–14th centuries); and Venantius Fortunatus (Carmina VII.18, 6th century) on runes painted on ash tablets for letters."},{use:"Amuletic formulas",evidence:"alu, laþu, laukaz, auja (“luck”) and gibu auja (“I give luck”) on 5th–6th-century bracteates and other objects; their sense is disputed."},{use:"Curses and the protection of monuments",evidence:"Björketorp and Stentoften (Blekinge, 6th–7th centuries); the Danish stones that ask Thor to hallow the runes or the monument, such as Glavendrup (DR 209, early 10th century)."},{use:"Healing charms",evidence:"The Ribe skull fragment (c. 725; its reading is debated), the Sigtuna amulet I (11th century), medieval lead amulets — and, in literature, Egils saga ch. 72 (Íslenzk fornrit II)."},{use:"Christian and learned charms written in runes",evidence:"Latin prayers, the SATOR square and AGLA on medieval runic amulets and sticks, such as Bryggen N 636: the overlap with the Latin and Hebrew magical traditions of the rest of the wing."},{use:"Eddic “rune-craft”",evidence:"Sigrdrífumál 6–13 names victory-, ale-, birth-, sea-, limb-, speech- and mind-runes and where to carve each, and stanza 18 says the runes were shaved off and mixed into the holy mead — the scraping-off Egill performs in his saga. Hávamál 144 asks whether the reader knows how to carve, interpret, colour, test, ask, offer, send and sacrifice (stanzas in Neckel–Kuhn’s numbering). Literary and late, but the main textual source for a magical understanding of runes."},{use:"Mnemonic and poetic use of the rune names",evidence:"The three rune poems; Cynewulf’s runic signatures; Old English riddles that use runes."},{use:"Cryptography and calendars",evidence:"The cipher runes of the Rök stone (early 9th century) and the medieval Icelandic and Scandinavian traditions of coded runes built on the three ættir; perpetual calendars on staves and boards, medieval and early modern, that use the sixteen Younger runes and three extra signs — arlaug, tvímaðr and belgþor — for the nineteen golden numbers."},{use:"Divination",evidence:"There is no ancient evidence for divination with individual runes; Tacitus’s lots (Germania 10) carried unspecified signs. Present-day rune divination draws on 20th-century writers: List (1908) gave each rune an esoteric meaning, and Blum (1982) popularised casting rune tiles."},{use:"Learned esotericism and modern occultism",evidence:"Johannes Bureus’s Adulruna (17th century); List’s Armanen runes (1908) and the völkisch rune-occultists of the 1920s and 1930s; post-war Heathen and occult use, reported here and not reproduced."}],quotations:[{text:"Veit ek, at ek hekk / vindga meiði á / nætr allar níu, / geiri undaðr / ok gefinn Óðni, / sjálfr sjálfum mér, / á þeim meiði, / er manngi veit / hvers af rótum renn.",language:"Old Norse",translation:"paraphrase",paraphrase:"I know that I hung on the wind-swept tree all of nine nights, wounded with a spear and given to Óðinn, myself to myself, on that tree of which no one knows from what roots it rises.",source:"Hávamál 138 (Neckel–Kuhn numbering), Codex Regius, GKS 2365 4to",note:"In normalised spelling, checked against Pettit’s dual-language edition (2023). Henry Adams Bellows, The Poetic Edda (New York: American-Scandinavian Foundation, 1923), numbers the stanza 139 and renders it, in the Project Gutenberg transcription: “I ween that I hung | on the windy tree, / Hung there for nights full nine; / With the spear I was wounded, | and offered I was / To Othin, myself to myself, / On the tree that none | may ever know / What root beneath it runs.”"},{text:"Við hleifi mik sældu / né við hornigi; / nýsta ek niðr, / nam ek upp rúnar, / æpandi nam, / fell ek aptr þaðan.",language:"Old Norse",translation:"paraphrase",paraphrase:"They comforted me with no loaf and no horn; I peered down; I took up the runes, screaming I took them; I fell back from there.",source:"Hávamál 139 (Neckel–Kuhn), Codex Regius, GKS 2365 4to",note:"Bellows (1923) numbers it 140: “None made me happy | with loaf or horn, / And there below I looked; / I took up the runes, | shrieking I took them, / And forthwith back I fell.”"},{text:"Veiztu hvé rísta skal? / Veiztu hvé ráða skal? / Veiztu hvé fá skal? / Veiztu hvé freista skal? / Veiztu hvé biðja skal? / Veiztu hvé blóta skal? / Veiztu hvé senda skal? / Veiztu hvé sóa skal?",language:"Old Norse",translation:"paraphrase",paraphrase:"Do you know how to carve? how to interpret? how to colour? how to test? how to ask? how to offer? how to send? how to sacrifice?",source:"Hávamál 144 (Neckel–Kuhn), Codex Regius, GKS 2365 4to",note:"Some editions spell Veistu. Bellows (1923) numbers it 145: “Knowest how one shall write, | knowest how one shall rede? / Knowest how one shall tint, | knowest how one makes trial? / Knowest how one shall ask, | knowest how one shall offer? / Knowest how one shall send, | knowest how one shall sacrifice?”"},{text:"Sigrúnar skaltu kunna, / ef þú vilt sigr hafa, / ok rísta á hjalti hjǫrs, / sumar á véttrimum, / sumar á valbǫstum, / ok nefna tysvar Tý.",language:"Old Norse",translation:"paraphrase",paraphrase:"Victory-runes you must know if you want victory, and carve them on the sword’s hilt, some on the blade-guards, some on the blade, and name Týr twice.",source:"Sigrdrífumál 6 (Neckel–Kuhn), Codex Regius",note:"The two words rendered “blade-guards” and “blade” are of uncertain meaning."},{text:"Ǫlrúnar skaltu kunna, / ef þú vill annars kvæn / véli-t þik í tryggð, ef þú trúir; / á horni skal þær rísta / ok á handar baki / ok merkja á nagli Nauð.",language:"Old Norse",translation:"paraphrase",paraphrase:"Ale-runes you must know if you do not want another man’s wife to betray your trust; carve them on the horn and on the back of the hand, and mark Nauð — the n-rune — on the nail.",source:"Sigrdrífumál 7 (Neckel–Kuhn), Codex Regius"},{text:"Full skal signa / ok við fári sjá / ok verpa lauk í lǫg.",language:"Old Norse",translation:"paraphrase",paraphrase:"The cup must be blessed, and guarded against harm, and a leek thrown into the drink.",source:"Sigrdrífumál 8 (Neckel–Kuhn), Codex Regius",note:"The literary parallel to laukaz on the bracteates. Some editions read lauki í lǫg."},{text:"Þurs ríst ek þér / ok þrjá stafi, / ergi ok æði / ok óþola; / svá ek þat af ríst, / sem ek þat á reist, / ef gørask þarfar þess.",language:"Old Norse",translation:"paraphrase",paraphrase:"A þurs — the þ-rune — I carve for you, and three staves: lust and frenzy and unbearable longing. I can carve it off as I carved it on, if there is need.",source:"Skírnismál 36 (Neckel–Kuhn), Codex Regius",note:"The manuscript order of this passage is disturbed and editors divide its stanzas differently, so the number varies between editions."},{text:"Skalat maðr rúnar rísta, / nema ráða vel kunni; …",language:"Old Norse",translation:"paraphrase",paraphrase:"A man should not carve runes unless he knows well how to read them.",source:"Egils saga Skalla-Grímssonar, ch. 72 (Íslenzk fornrit II, ed. Sigurður Nordal, 1933); Skaldic Project, Egill Skalla-Grímsson, Lausavísur 40",note:"The opening of the verse the saga gives to Egill at the whalebone; further on it calls the sick girl lauka lindi, “linden of leeks”. Only these words could be checked against the Skaldic Project edition, so the rest of the verse is not given. Published English versions, in copyright and not quoted: Hermann Pálsson and Paul Edwards (Penguin, 1976); Bernard Scudder, in The Complete Sagas of Icelanders I (1997)."},{text:"Feoh byþ frofur fira gehwylcum; / sceal ðeah manna gehwylc miclun hyt dælan / gif he wile for drihtne domes hleotan.",language:"Old English",translation:"paraphrase",paraphrase:"Wealth is a comfort to everyone; yet each must share it out freely if he wants to win glory before the Lord.",source:"Old English Rune Poem, stanza 1 (feoh), from Hickes’s Thesaurus (1703–05)",note:"Bruce Dickins’s translation (1915) is the standard English rendering; it is not quoted here."},{text:"virgam frugiferae arbori decisam in surculos amputant eosque notis quibusdam discretos super candidam vestem temere ac fortuito spargunt.",language:"Latin",translation:"paraphrase",paraphrase:"They cut a branch from a fruit-bearing tree into slips, mark them with certain signs, and scatter them at random over a white cloth.",source:"Tacitus, Germania 10 (98 CE)",note:"The signs are not said to be runes. A published English version: M. Hutton, revised E. H. Warmington, Loeb Classical Library (1970)."},{text:"runo fahi raginakudo …",language:"Proto-Norse",translation:"paraphrase",paraphrase:"I paint — that is, write — the rune derived from the gods.",source:"The Noleby stone, Västergötland (Rundata Vg 63), c. 600",note:"Some transcribe raginaku(n)do."},{text:"izwis atgiban ist kunnan runa þiudangardjos gudis",language:"Gothic",translation:"paraphrase",paraphrase:"To you it is given to know the mystery (runa) of the kingdom of God.",source:"Wulfila’s Gothic Bible (Codex Argenteus), Mark 4:11",note:"The passage shows Gothic rūna answering to the Greek mystērion."},{text:"barbara fraxineis pingatur runa tabellis, / quodque papyrus agit, virgula plana valet.",language:"Latin",translation:"paraphrase",paraphrase:"Let the barbarian rune be painted on tablets of ash; what papyrus does, a smoothed stick can do.",source:"Venantius Fortunatus, Carmina VII.18 (to Flavus), 6th century",note:"Evidence for runes as an ordinary means of correspondence. Some editions print rhuna for runa."}],links:[{to:"latin",relation:"One of the three candidate parents (Wimmer, 1874). Medieval Scandinavians wrote Latin prayers and charms in runes, and Ole Worm and Bureus made rune study part of Latin learning."},{to:"greek",relation:"Another proposed parent (Bugge; von Friesen), now generally rejected on chronology. Gothic rūna translates the Greek mystērion."},{to:"phoenician",relation:"The remote ancestor, through Greek and the Etruscan or North Italic alphabets."},{to:"hebrew",relation:"AGLA, a notarikon of the Hebrew Atah gibbor le-olam Adonai, appears on medieval runic amulets, and Bureus’s Adulruna drew on Christian Kabbalah: here the runic tradition meets the Hebrew-based magical alphabets."},{to:"theban",relation:"Both turn up in modern lists of “magical alphabets” and in modern witchcraft, but their histories are unrelated. Theban is a cipher alphabet keyed to Latin, first printed in Trithemius’s Polygraphia (1518), which ascribes it to Honorius of Thebes on the authority of Pietro d’Abano, and reprinted by Agrippa in De occulta philosophia III.29; the runes are a native script."},{to:"galdrastafir",relation:"The Icelandic magical staves, in the Galdrabók (16th–17th century) and the Huld manuscript (1847 or 1860), sometimes include runes, but they are sign-magic, not runes. The vegvísir is first known from the Huld manuscript; the name ægishjálmur occurs in the Eddic Fáfnismál, but the drawn sign comes from late manuscripts. Neither sign dates from the Viking Age."},{to:"charakteres",relation:"Contrast only: the charaktêres are signs, not letters, and the Icelandic staves that sometimes include runes belong with them as sign-magic, not with the runes."}],cautions:["The research for this page was fact-checked against Rundata, museum and library records and published editions where they could be reached; the Unicode code points and names were checked against the Unicode Character Database, and the letter descriptions against the outlines of the font. Still unverified, and so not stated here: where the single- and double-barred h occur in the early inscriptions; the Latin and “chieftain” glosses of the Icelandic Rune Poem; which SS offices and units used the odal rune; the further etymologies proposed for alu, and the alu stamps reported from Spong Hill; the place and publisher of List’s book of 1908; and whether the blank rune is in the first edition of Blum.","Stanza and chapter numbers differ between editions. Hávamál 138 in Neckel–Kuhn is 139 in Bellows, who moves the manuscript’s closing stanza ahead of the Rúnatal, and Bellows also orders stanzas 79 and 80 differently. Egils saga chapters here follow Íslenzk fornrit II (the horn ch. 44, the scorn-pole ch. 57, the whalebone ch. 72); W. C. Green’s translation of 1893 puts the horn in ch. 45. Each number here names its edition.","The Proto-Germanic names (*fehu, *ansuz …) are reconstructions. Only the rune-poem and manuscript names are attested, and those are medieval.","The modern divinatory meanings, the blank rune, the Armanen row, “rune yoga” and the vegvísir are modern, and are reported here with their dates, not as ancient.","The runes appear here only as letters in their historical row, in a neutral typeface. Their use as insignia is reported in its own place and not reproduced; German courts apply §86a to look-alike forms too, as the Bavarian Higher Regional Court did in June 2026 with a Sig-rune-style letter on a demonstration poster (BayObLG 206 StRR 137/26, as reported by beck-aktuell).","Modern Heathen religious bodies and rune-occult orders may be named, but their emblems and texts are not reproduced.","The Codex Regius poems (c. 1270) and Egils saga (c. 1220–40) were written in Christian Iceland centuries after the Elder Futhark went out of use. They are evidence for medieval ideas about runes, not for Migration-Period practice.","Stephen E. Flowers, cited here for the formula words of the early inscriptions (Runes and Magic, 1986), also writes on rune practice as Edred Thorsson; on interpretation his work is read beside the sceptical runologists, Page and Barnes."],sources:[{citation:"Michael P. Barnes, Runes: A Handbook (Woodbridge: Boydell, 2012)."},{citation:"Klaus Düwel, Runenkunde, Sammlung Metzler 72, 4th ed. (Stuttgart: Metzler, 2008)."},{citation:"R. I. Page, Runes, Reading the Past (London: British Museum Press, 1987)."},{citation:"R. I. Page, An Introduction to English Runes (London: Methuen, 1973; 2nd ed. Woodbridge: Boydell, 1999)."},{citation:"R. I. Page, “Anglo-Saxon Runes and Magic”, Journal of the British Archaeological Association, 3rd ser., 27 (1964); repr. in Page, Runes and Runic Inscriptions, ed. David Parsons (Woodbridge: Boydell, 1995)."},{citation:"R. I. Page, The Icelandic Rune-Poem (1999), an edition of AM 687d 4to."},{citation:"Terje Spurkland, Norwegian Runes and Runic Inscriptions, trans. Betsy van der Hoek (Woodbridge: Boydell, 2005)."},{citation:"Tineke Looijenga, Texts and Contexts of the Oldest Runic Inscriptions (Leiden: Brill, 2003)."},{citation:"Mindy MacLeod and Bernard Mees, Runic Amulets and Magic Objects (Woodbridge: Boydell, 2006)."},{citation:"Stephen E. Flowers, Runes and Magic: Magical Formulaic Elements in the Older Runic Tradition (New York: Peter Lang, 1986)."},{citation:"Wolfgang Krause with Herbert Jankuhn, Die Runeninschriften im älteren Futhark (Göttingen, 1966)."},{citation:"Samnordisk runtextdatabas (Rundata), Uppsala University: the standard corpus and signa; the record for DR 207, the Vimose comb.",url:"https://rundata.info/inscription/dr-207"},{citation:"Rundata, N KJ31: the Øvre Stabu spearhead, dated 160–250/260 (Imer 2007).",url:"https://rundata.info/inscription/n-kj31/"},{citation:"Rundata, N 636 (Bryggen, Bergen): AGLA, SATOR AREPO and the names of the archangels.",url:"https://rundata.info/inscription/n-636/"},{citation:"Museum of Cultural History, University of Oslo, announcement of the Svingerud runestone, January 2023 (radiocarbon 1–250 CE), as reported by sciencenorway.no.",url:"https://sciencenorway.no/archaeology-runes/the-worlds-oldest-rune-stone-was-found-sticking-out-of-the-ground-heres-how-researchers-figured-out-how-old-it-is/2148579"},{citation:"“Inscribed sandstone fragments of Hole, Norway: radiocarbon dates provide insight into rune stone traditions”, Antiquity (2025).",url:"https://www.antiquity.ac.uk/index.php/news/2025/piecing-together-puzzle-worlds-oldest-datable-rune-stone"},{citation:"Hanna Åkerström, “Läsordningen på Stentoftenstenen”, Futhark: International Journal of Runic Studies (2025).",url:"https://journals.uu.se/futhark/article/view/1091"},{citation:"Bernard Mees, “The Noleby inscription and Germanic *rehaną”, Arkiv för nordisk filologi.",url:"https://journals.lub.lu.se/anf/article/download/27784/24232"},{citation:"Jackie Nordström, “Dvärgen på Ribekraniet”, Arkiv för nordisk filologi (2021).",url:"https://journals.lub.lu.se/anf/article/view/27870"},{citation:"Staffan Fridell, “The dwarfs at the Ribe skull” (a response to Nordström), Arkiv för nordisk filologi (2024).",url:"https://journals.lub.lu.se/anf/en/article/view/28741"},{citation:"Alaric Hall, “Þur sarriþu þursa trutin: monster-fighting and medicine in early medieval Scandinavia”, Asclepio (2009), on the Sigtuna amulet I.",url:"https://alarichall.org.uk/teaching/articledraft/asclepio_article_day4.pdf"},{citation:"National Museum of Denmark, “Runic magic”: the reading of Glavendrup (DR 209).",url:"https://en.natmus.dk/historical-knowledge/denmark/prehistoric-period-until-1050-ad/the-viking-age/religion-magic-death-and-rituals/runic-magic/"},{citation:"British Museum, the Franks Casket, museum no. 1867,0120.1.",url:"https://britishmuseum.org/collection/object/H_1867-0120-1"},{citation:"British Museum, the Seax of Beagnoth (Thames scramasax), 10th century.",url:"https://www.britishmuseum.org/explore/highlights/highlight_objects/pe_mla/s/seax_of_beagnoth.aspx"},{citation:"George Hickes, Linguarum veterum septentrionalium thesaurus (Oxford, 1703–05): the only witness to the Old English Rune Poem after the Cotton fire of 1731."},{citation:"Bruce Dickins, Runic and Heroic Poems of the Old Teutonic Peoples (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1915)."},{citation:"Maureen Halsall, The Old English Rune Poem: A Critical Edition (Toronto: University of Toronto Press, 1981)."},{citation:"“The Norwegian Rune Poem in context: structure, style and imagery” (Università di Cagliari repository): its date and witnesses.",url:"https://iris.unica.it/retrieve/e2f56ed7-e790-3eaf-e053-3a05fe0a5d97/The-Norwegian-Rune-Poem-in-context-structure-style-and-imagery.pdf"},{citation:"Gustav Neckel and Hans Kuhn (eds), Edda: Die Lieder des Codex Regius nebst verwandten Denkmälern, 5th ed. (Heidelberg: Winter, 1983)."},{citation:"Henry Adams Bellows (trans.), The Poetic Edda (New York: American-Scandinavian Foundation, 1923), Project Gutenberg no. 73533.",url:"https://www.gutenberg.org/files/73533/73533-h/73533-h.htm"},{citation:"Edward Pettit (ed. and trans.), The Poetic Edda: A Dual-Language Edition (Cambridge: Open Book Publishers, 2023), Hávamál.",url:"https://books.openbookpublishers.com/10.11647/obp.0308/ch2.xhtml"},{citation:"Sigurður Nordal (ed.), Egils saga Skalla-Grímssonar, Íslenzk fornrit II (Reykjavík, 1933)."},{citation:"Egil’s Saga, trans. Hermann Pálsson and Paul Edwards (Harmondsworth: Penguin, 1976); trans. Bernard Scudder, in The Complete Sagas of Icelanders I (Reykjavík: Leifur Eiríksson, 1997)."},{citation:"Skaldic Poetry of the Scandinavian Middle Ages (Skaldic Project), Egill Skalla-Grímsson, Lausavísur 40.",url:"https://skaldic.org/m.php?p=verse&i=2305"},{citation:"Tacitus, Germania, trans. M. Hutton, rev. E. H. Warmington, Loeb Classical Library 35 (1970)."},{citation:"Venantius Fortunatus, Carmina VII.18, ed. F. Leo, Monumenta Germaniae Historica, Auctores antiquissimi 4.1 (Berlin, 1881); the Latin text in Musisque Deoque.",url:"https://www.mqdq.it/testo/testo/ordinata/pf2109018"},{citation:"Wulfila Project, the Gothic Bible, Mark chapter 4.",url:"https://wulfila.be/gothic/browse/text/04/04.html"},{citation:"Thomas Karlsson, Götisk kabbala och runisk alkemi: Johannes Bureus och den götiska esoterismen (doctoral dissertation, Stockholm University, 2010)."},{citation:"Susanna Åkerman, Rose Cross over the Baltic: The Spread of Rosicrucianism in Northern Europe (Leiden: Brill, 1998), on Bureus."},{citation:"“Kabbalah in Sweden” (ResearchGate publication 326887954), on Bureus’s Kabbalistic sources.",url:"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/326887954_Kabbalah_in_Sweden"},{citation:"Ole Worm, Runir seu Danica literatura antiquissima (Copenhagen, 1636; 2nd ed. 1651); Danicorum monumentorum libri sex (Copenhagen, 1643)."},{citation:"Guido von List, Das Geheimnis der Runen, Guido-von-List-Bücherei 1 (1908; first printed as a periodical article, 1906); trans. Stephen E. Flowers, The Secret of the Runes (Rochester, VT: Destiny Books, 1988)."},{citation:"Nicholas Goodrick-Clarke, The Occult Roots of Nazism (Wellingborough: Aquarian Press, 1985; New York University Press, 1992)."},{citation:"Bernard Mees, The Science of the Swastika (Budapest: Central European University Press, 2008)."},{citation:"Deutscher Bundestag, Wissenschaftliche Dienste, “Das strafbare Verwenden von Kennzeichen verfassungswidriger Organisationen” (2021).",url:"https://www.bundestag.de/resource/blob/869290/c8bd5f14ef172eb76e41484886611030/Das-strafbare-Verw-von-Kennzeichen-data.pdf"},{citation:"Ralph Blum, The Book of Runes (New York: Oracle Books / St. Martin’s Press, 1982; some records give 1983)."},{citation:"The Unicode Standard, Runic block, U+16A0–U+16FF (Unicode 3.0, 1999; U+16F1–16F8 in Unicode 7.0, 2014)."}]};export{e as default};
